window.BioChecks[29] = {"2004-Q12a":[{"q":"What is homeostasis, and what is the role of the kidneys in it? (3 points)","right":"Keeping internal environment constant; kidneys balance water and salt","wrong":["Keeping internal environment constant; kidneys make urea from protein","Keeping internal environment constant; kidneys release insulin","Removing undigested food; kidneys balance water and salt"],"why":"Three points: homeostasis is keeping a constant internal environment; the kidneys keep the body's water balance and salt balance steady."}],"2004-Q12b":[{"q":"On a nephron diagram, which vessel carries blood into the glomerulus?","right":"Afferent arteriole","wrong":["Efferent arteriole","Renal venule","Peritubular capillary"],"why":"The afferent arteriole brings blood in (a for arrive); the efferent arteriole takes it out (e for exit)."},{"q":"How do filtration and reabsorption occur in the nephron? (6 points)","right":"Pressure: plasma into capsule; osmosis from tubule to blood","wrong":["Pressure: proteins into capsule; osmosis from tubule to blood","Pressure: plasma into ureter; osmosis from blood to tubule","Low pressure: cells into capsule; osmosis from tubule to urine"],"why":"Three points each: high pressure forces plasma (not cells or proteins) into Bowman's capsule; the tubule returns water to the blood by osmosis."}],"2005-Q1f":[{"q":"Getting rid of the waste made by metabolism is called what?","right":"Excretion","wrong":["Egestion","Secretion","Deamination"],"why":"Excretion removes waste cells make, like urea and CO2; egestion removes undigested food that cells never made."}],"2005-Q3a":[{"q":"True or false: urea is made in the kidneys.","right":"False","wrong":["True"],"why":"Urea is made in the liver by deamination; the kidneys only filter it out of the blood."}],"2006-Q6b":[{"q":"Distinguish between the ureter and the urethra. (2 points)","right":"Ureter: kidney to bladder; urethra: bladder to outside","wrong":["Ureter: bladder to outside; urethra: kidney to bladder","Ureter: kidney to bladder; urethra: bladder to kidney","Ureter: blood to kidney; urethra: bladder to outside"],"why":"Two points: the ureter carries urine from the kidney to the bladder; the urethra carries it from the bladder out of the body."}],"2006-Q13b":[{"q":"Explain the terms plasma and glomerular filtrate. (2 points)","right":"Plasma: liquid part of blood; filtrate: plasma minus its proteins","wrong":["Plasma: liquid part of blood; filtrate: plasma with all its proteins","Plasma: red cells of the blood; filtrate: plasma minus its proteins","Plasma: liquid part of blood; filtrate: urine stored in the bladder"],"why":"Two points: plasma is the liquid part of blood; glomerular filtrate is plasma, minus its proteins, that has passed into Bowman's capsule."},{"q":"Why are red blood cells missing from glomerular filtrate?","right":"They are too big to pass into the capsule","wrong":["They are reabsorbed in the proximal tubule","They are broken down in the glomerulus","ADH stops them leaving the blood vessels"],"why":"Red cells are far too big to squeeze through the glomerulus wall into Bowman's capsule, so they stay in the blood."},{"q":"Why does filtrate have the same glucose level as plasma?","right":"Glucose is small and passes freely through","wrong":["Glucose is pumped in by active transport","Insulin pushes glucose into the capsule","Glucose is made by cells of the capsule"],"why":"Glucose molecules are small enough to be filtered freely, so filtrate gets the same glucose level as plasma."},{"q":"Why is there normally no glucose in urine?","right":"It is all reabsorbed into the blood","wrong":["It is too big to pass into the capsule","It is turned into urea in the kidney","ADH stops it from being filtered"],"why":"Glucose is filtered, but the proximal tubule reabsorbs all of it back into the blood, so none reaches the urine."},{"q":"After heavy exercise, why is a small volume of concentrated urine made? (one of 4 points)","right":"More ADH, so more water is reabsorbed","wrong":["More ADH, so less water is reabsorbed","Less ADH, so less water is reabsorbed","More insulin, so more water is reabsorbed"],"why":"Four of: sweating dehydrates you; the brain detects it; the pituitary secretes ADH; more water is reabsorbed in the collecting duct."}],"2007-Q15c":[{"q":"What is homeostasis, and why is it important in the body? (3 points)","right":"Maintaining a constant internal environment, so enzymes work well","wrong":["Maintaining a constant internal environment, so no excretion is needed","Letting conditions inside match the outside, so enzymes work well","Removing undigested food from the gut, so enzymes work well"],"why":"Three points: homeostasis is the maintenance of a constant internal environment, which keeps conditions right for enzymes to work."}],"2008-Q13a":[{"q":"Which best describes excretion?","right":"Removing waste made by the body's metabolism","wrong":["Removing undigested food from the gut","Releasing useful substances such as hormones","Breaking down large food molecules"],"why":"Excretion gets rid of waste made by cells, like urea and CO2; removing undigested food is egestion."},{"q":"Name a substance urea is made from, and one carbon dioxide is made from. (2 points)","right":"Urea from amino acids; carbon dioxide from glucose","wrong":["Urea from fatty acids; carbon dioxide from glucose","Urea from amino acids; carbon dioxide from water","Urea from glycerol; carbon dioxide from water"],"why":"Two points: urea comes from amino acids (protein) broken down in the liver; CO2 comes from respiring glucose (carbohydrate) or fat."}],"2008-Q13b":[{"q":"On the diagram, which part is C?","right":"Proximal convoluted tubule","wrong":["Distal convoluted tubule","Collecting duct","Loop of Henle"],"why":"C comes straight after Bowman's capsule, so it is the proximal (first) tubule; the distal one comes after the loop."},{"q":"Arteriole A branches from which blood vessel?","right":"Renal artery","wrong":["Renal vein","Efferent arteriole","Inferior vena cava"],"why":"The renal artery brings blood into the kidney and branches into the arterioles that feed each glomerulus."},{"q":"In which region of the kidney is Bowman's capsule (B)?","right":"Cortex","wrong":["Medulla","Pelvis","Pyramid"],"why":"Bowman's capsules and glomeruli sit in the outer cortex; the loops of Henle dip down into the medulla."},{"q":"In which part of the nephron are amino acids reabsorbed?","right":"Proximal convoluted tubule","wrong":["Distal convoluted tubule","Bowman's capsule","Collecting duct"],"why":"The proximal tubule reabsorbs all the glucose and amino acids; later parts mainly handle water and salts."},{"q":"Give two features of the nephron that aid filtration. (2 points)","right":"Efferent arteriole narrower than afferent; walls one cell thick","wrong":["Afferent arteriole narrower than efferent; walls one cell thick","Efferent arteriole narrower than afferent; thick muscular walls","Small surface area; few, wide capillaries in the glomerulus"],"why":"Two of: efferent arteriole narrower than afferent (raises pressure), capillary walls one cell thick or porous, a large surface area."},{"q":"Which group of biomolecules is too big to be filtered?","right":"Proteins","wrong":["Glucose","Amino acids","Urea"],"why":"Proteins are big molecules that can't pass through the glomerulus wall; glucose and amino acids are small."}],"2008-Q13c":[{"q":"Suggest two situations that may cause a drop in the water content of the blood. (2 points)","right":"Heavy sweating in hot weather, and not drinking enough water","wrong":["Heavy sweating in hot weather, and drinking lots of extra water","More ADH from the pituitary, and not drinking enough water","Drinking lots of extra water, and releasing more ADH"],"why":"Two of: sweating in heat or exercise, low water intake, high salt intake, an infection, or taking diuretics."},{"q":"Name the hormone released when blood water drops, and the gland that secretes it. (2 points)","right":"ADH, from the pituitary gland","wrong":["ADH, from the adrenal gland","Insulin, from the pituitary gland","Adrenaline, from the thyroid gland"],"why":"Two points: the hormone is ADH (anti-diuretic hormone), and it is secreted by the pituitary gland at the base of the brain."},{"q":"Give a precise target area for ADH, and say how it reaches it. (2 points)","right":"The collecting duct; it travels there in the blood","wrong":["Bowman's capsule; it travels there in the blood","The collecting duct; it travels there along a nerve","The glomerulus; it travels there down the ureter"],"why":"Two points: ADH acts on the collecting duct (or distal tubule), and it reaches it in the bloodstream, like all hormones."},{"q":"When blood water is low, what does ADH do at its target?","right":"Walls more permeable, so more water is reabsorbed","wrong":["Walls less permeable, so more water stays in urine","Walls more permeable, so more glucose is reabsorbed","Walls less permeable, so less water is reabsorbed"],"why":"ADH makes the duct walls more permeable to water, so more water moves back into the blood. The scheme wants both halves."}],"2009-Q15c":[{"q":"Write a short note on homeostasis. (3 points)","right":"Maintaining a constant internal environment, e.g. sweating to cool","wrong":["Maintaining a constant internal environment, e.g. shivering to cool","Letting conditions inside match the outside, e.g. sweating to cool","Removing undigested food from the gut, e.g. egesting faeces"],"why":"Three points: maintenance of a constant internal environment, plus an example of how, like sweating to lose heat when hot."}],"2010-Q15c":[{"q":"Which chain explains the athlete's concentrated urine?","right":"Sweat loss, more ADH, more water reabsorbed","wrong":["Sweat loss, less ADH, more water reabsorbed","Sweat loss, more ADH, less water reabsorbed","Extra urea, less ADH, less water reabsorbed"],"why":"Losing water in sweat leaves less water in the blood, so more ADH is released and the ducts reabsorb more water."}],"2011-Q12a":[{"q":"What does excretion mean?","right":"Getting rid of waste made by body cells","wrong":["Getting rid of food that was never digested","Releasing enzymes and hormones from glands","Breaking food down into small molecules"],"why":"Excretion removes wastes your own cells make, like urea; getting rid of undigested food is egestion."}],"2011-Q12b":[{"q":"On a kidney section, which region collects urine before the ureter?","right":"Pelvis","wrong":["Cortex","Medulla","Pyramid"],"why":"The pelvis is the hollow space in the middle where urine collects before draining into the ureter."},{"q":"On a kidney section, where does reabsorption happen?","right":"In the tubules of the cortex and medulla","wrong":["In the pelvis, where urine collects","In the ureter, leaving the kidney","In the renal vein, leaving the kidney"],"why":"Reabsorption happens in the nephron tubules, found in the cortex and medulla; the pelvis and ureter only carry urine."},{"q":"The renal artery branches off which blood vessel?","right":"Aorta","wrong":["Vena cava","Pulmonary artery","Hepatic artery"],"why":"The renal arteries branch straight off the aorta, the main artery from the heart; the vena cava is a vein."},{"q":"In which body cavity are the kidneys?","right":"Abdominal cavity","wrong":["Thoracic cavity","Pelvic cavity","Pleural cavity"],"why":"The kidneys sit at the back of the abdomen, below the diaphragm; the thoracic cavity holds the heart and lungs."},{"q":"Which substance, other than water, is excreted in urine?","right":"Urea","wrong":["Glucose","Protein","Amino acids"],"why":"Urea, made from excess amino acids in the liver, leaves in urine; glucose and amino acids are normally reabsorbed."},{"q":"What shows the kidney works like an exocrine gland?","right":"It releases its product through a duct","wrong":["It releases hormones into the blood","It has a very rich blood supply","It filters blood under high pressure"],"why":"Exocrine glands release their products through ducts; urine leaves the kidney through a duct, the ureter."}],"2011-Q12c":[{"q":"On the diagram, what is part 4? (one of 8 points)","right":"Efferent arteriole","wrong":["Afferent arteriole","Renal venule","Renal artery"],"why":"Part 4 takes blood out (e for exit). 8 points: parts 1-6 (capsule, glomerulus, afferent, efferent, PCT, DCT), filtration at 1 or 2, and ADH."},{"q":"Is protein in urine normal, and why?","right":"No, proteins are too big to be filtered","wrong":["Yes, proteins are small enough to filter","Yes, proteins are a normal waste product","No, ADH stops proteins being filtered"],"why":"Protein molecules are too big to pass through the glomerulus wall, so protein in urine suggests kidney damage."},{"q":"Is glucose in urine normal, and why?","right":"No, all glucose should be reabsorbed","wrong":["No, glucose is too big to be filtered","Yes, extra glucose is a waste product","Yes, glucose is never reabsorbed"],"why":"Glucose is filtered, but the proximal tubule should reabsorb all of it; glucose in urine can be a sign of diabetes."}],"2012-Q15c":[{"q":"What is homeostasis?","right":"Keeping the internal environment constant","wrong":["Removing the waste products of metabolism","Keeping the outside environment constant","Producing hormones to control growth"],"why":"Homeostasis keeps conditions inside the body, like temperature, pH and water, steady; removing waste is excretion."},{"q":"How does the liver help homeostasis?","right":"Stores or releases glucose as needed","wrong":["Filters urea out of the blood into urine","Excretes carbon dioxide from the body","Secretes insulin to lower blood sugar"],"why":"The liver stores glucose as glycogen and releases it when needed; insulin comes from the pancreas, not the liver."}],"2016-Q12a":[{"q":"Distinguish between excretion and egestion. (2 points)","right":"Excretion removes metabolic waste; egestion removes undigested food","wrong":["Excretion removes undigested food; egestion removes metabolic waste","Excretion removes metabolic waste; egestion releases gut enzymes","Excretion releases hormones; egestion removes undigested food"],"why":"Two points: excretion removes waste made by metabolism (like urea); egestion removes undigested food that was never absorbed."}],"2016-Q12b":[{"q":"Name two excretory products of mammals, other than water. (2 points)","right":"Carbon dioxide and urea","wrong":["Oxygen and urea","Carbon dioxide and fibre","Glucose and amino acids"],"why":"Two of: carbon dioxide (from respiration), urea (from extra amino acids) and salts. Fibre is egested, not excreted."},{"q":"Where in the body are carbon dioxide and urea produced? (2 points)","right":"CO2 in all body cells (respiration); urea in the liver","wrong":["CO2 only in the lungs (breathing); urea in the liver","CO2 in all body cells (respiration); urea in the kidneys","CO2 in the liver (deamination); urea in the bladder"],"why":"Two points: CO2 is made in all body cells by respiration; urea is made in the liver by deamination. Lungs and kidneys only remove them."},{"q":"Describe the role of ADH in excretion. (3 points)","right":"Collecting ducts more permeable, more water reabsorbed, less urine","wrong":["Collecting ducts less permeable, less water reabsorbed, more urine","Collecting ducts more permeable, more salt reabsorbed, more urine","Glomerulus more permeable, more water filtered, more urine"],"why":"Three of: released when dehydrated, acts on collecting ducts, makes their walls more permeable, more water reabsorbed, less urine made."}],"2016-Q12c":[{"q":"What does the term homeostasis mean?","right":"Maintaining a steady internal environment","wrong":["Removing undigested food from the body","Letting inside conditions match outside","Breaking down excess amino acids"],"why":"Homeostasis keeps the inside of the body steady (temperature, pH, water) even when the outside changes."}],"2016-Q15a":[{"q":"Which vein carries blood away from the kidneys?","right":"Renal vein","wrong":["Renal artery","Hepatic vein","Hepatic portal vein"],"why":"The renal vein drains blood from each kidney to the vena cava; the renal artery brings blood in."}],"2017-Q14c":[{"q":"How is the glomerulus suited to filtering blood? (2 points)","right":"High blood pressure, and capillary walls only one cell thick","wrong":["High blood pressure, and thick muscular walls to resist it","Low blood pressure, and capillary walls only one cell thick","A small surface area, and thick walls that stop leaks"],"why":"Two of: high blood pressure, capillary walls one cell thick, a large surface area, and being a knot of capillaries."}],"2017-Q15a":[{"q":"Which phrase explains homeostasis?","right":"Keeping conditions inside the body steady","wrong":["Keeping conditions outside the body steady","Getting rid of waste made by cells","Breaking food down to release energy"],"why":"Homeostasis is about the internal environment staying constant; getting rid of cell waste is excretion."},{"q":"Why does the body need homeostasis?","right":"So enzymes get the right temperature and pH","wrong":["So the body no longer needs to excrete","So blood glucose can rise freely after meals","So body temperature can match the weather"],"why":"Enzymes only work well in a narrow range of temperature and pH, so keeping these steady keeps metabolism going."}],"2018-Q6":[{"q":"On the diagram, which structure is Y?","right":"Distal convoluted tubule","wrong":["Proximal convoluted tubule","Collecting duct","Loop of Henle"],"why":"Y comes after the loop of Henle, so it is the distal (far) tubule; the proximal one is right after Bowman's capsule."},{"q":"Where on the diagram does ADH act?","right":"Collecting duct","wrong":["Glomerulus","Afferent arteriole","Proximal convoluted tubule"],"why":"ADH targets the collecting duct (and distal tubule), the last parts of the nephron, where water can be saved."},{"q":"What does ADH do to its target area?","right":"Makes it more permeable to water","wrong":["Makes it less permeable to water","Makes it pump glucose into urine","Makes it let protein into urine"],"why":"With ADH the duct walls let more water through, so more water is reabsorbed into the blood."},{"q":"What change in the blood triggers ADH release?","right":"Higher salt, less water in the blood","wrong":["Lower salt, more water in the blood","Higher blood volume after drinking","Higher carbon dioxide in the blood"],"why":"When blood gets too salty (too little water), the brain detects it and ADH is released to save water."},{"q":"In which gland is ADH made?","right":"Hypothalamus","wrong":["Adrenal gland","Thyroid gland","Pancreas"],"why":"ADH is made in the hypothalamus and released from the pituitary; the scheme accepts either."},{"q":"Which is a treatment for kidney failure?","right":"Kidney dialysis","wrong":["Insulin injections","A course of antibiotics","Blood transfusion"],"why":"A dialysis machine filters urea and extra salt from the blood when the kidneys fail; a transplant also works."}],"2020-Q13a":[{"q":"What is meant by homeostasis?","right":"Maintaining a stable internal environment","wrong":["Maintaining a stable external environment","Removing the waste of metabolism","Changing body temperature with the weather"],"why":"Homeostasis keeps the body's internal conditions steady; removing metabolic waste is excretion."},{"q":"Why is homeostasis important in organisms?","right":"Lets enzymes and metabolism work properly","wrong":["Stops cells from needing any energy","Lets body pH change freely with diet","Lets cells take in water without limit"],"why":"Enzymes need a steady temperature and pH, so a constant internal environment keeps metabolism running well."},{"q":"What is meant by excretion?","right":"Removal of metabolic waste from the body","wrong":["Removal of undigested food from the body","Keeping a constant internal environment","Release of energy from food in cells"],"why":"Excretion removes waste made by metabolism, like urea and CO2; removing undigested food is egestion."}],"2020-Q13b":[{"q":"Where in the body is urea made?","right":"Liver","wrong":["Kidney","Bladder","Pancreas"],"why":"The liver breaks down excess amino acids (deamination) to make urea; the kidneys only remove it."},{"q":"Which food biomolecule is urea made from?","right":"Protein","wrong":["Carbohydrate","Lipid","Vitamins"],"why":"Proteins contain nitrogen; extra amino acids are deaminated and the nitrogen ends up in urea. Carbs and fats have none."},{"q":"In which part of the kidney is the loop of Henle found?","right":"Medulla","wrong":["Cortex","Pelvis","Ureter"],"why":"The loops of Henle dip down into the medulla; Bowman's capsules and the convoluted tubules are in the cortex."},{"q":"In order, which parts follow Bowman's capsule in a nephron?","right":"PCT, loop of Henle, DCT, collecting duct","wrong":["DCT, loop of Henle, PCT, collecting duct","Loop of Henle, PCT, DCT, collecting duct","PCT, DCT, loop of Henle, collecting duct"],"why":"Filtrate flows from the capsule to the proximal tubule (PCT), down the loop of Henle, to the distal tubule (DCT)."}],"2020-Q13c":[{"q":"From the table, what is the main difference between plasma and filtrate, and why? (2 points)","right":"Plasma has protein, filtrate has none; protein is too big to pass","wrong":["Filtrate has protein, plasma has none; protein is made in the capsule","Plasma has protein, filtrate has none; ADH stops protein passing","Plasma has glucose, filtrate has none; glucose is too big to pass"],"why":"Two points: plasma has protein (7 g/100 cm3) but filtrate has none, because protein molecules are too big to pass out of the glomerulus."},{"q":"From the table, why is there glucose in filtrate but none in urine?","right":"All the glucose is reabsorbed into blood","wrong":["Glucose is too big to enter the filtrate","Glucose is turned into urea in the tubule","Glucose is used up by the bladder wall"],"why":"Glucose is filtered (0.1 in both plasma and filtrate), then all of it is reabsorbed in the tubule, so urine has 0."},{"q":"What does a high salt intake do to urine volume, and why?","right":"Less urine, as more ADH is released","wrong":["More urine, as more ADH is released","More urine, as less ADH is released","Less urine, as less ADH is released"],"why":"Salty blood triggers more ADH, so the collecting ducts reabsorb more water and less urine is made."}],"2021-Q15a":[{"q":"What does homeostasis mean?","right":"Keeping a stable internal environment","wrong":["Keeping the external environment stable","Removing toxic waste made by cells","Controlling growth and development"],"why":"Homeostasis means the body keeps its inside conditions, like temperature and water level, stable."}],"2022-Q15a":[{"q":"What do the terms excretion and homeostasis mean? (2 points)","right":"Removing metabolic waste; keeping a constant internal environment","wrong":["Removing undigested food; keeping a constant internal environment","Removing metabolic waste; letting conditions match the outside","Releasing hormones into blood; removing undigested food"],"why":"Two points: excretion is removing waste made by metabolism; homeostasis is keeping a constant internal environment."}],"2022-Q15b":[{"q":"What does the fat layer around the kidneys do?","right":"Protects them from knocks and bumps","wrong":["Stores urine before it goes to the bladder","Makes ADH to control water balance","Filters blood before it enters"],"why":"The fat cushions the kidneys against knocks (and insulates them); urine is stored in the bladder, not the fat."},{"q":"On the kidney diagram, which region is B?","right":"Medulla","wrong":["Cortex","Pelvis","Ureter"],"why":"B is the middle layer with the pyramids: the medulla. The cortex (A) is outside it and the pelvis (C) inside."},{"q":"In which kidney region does filtration happen?","right":"Cortex (A)","wrong":["Medulla (B)","Pelvis (C)","Ureter"],"why":"Filtration happens at the glomerulus and Bowman's capsule, which are found in the outer cortex."},{"q":"Why does blood enter the glomerulus under high pressure?","right":"To force small molecules out into the capsule","wrong":["To force proteins out into the capsule","To push filtrate back into the blood","To stop any plasma leaving the blood"],"why":"High pressure squeezes water and small molecules out of the blood into Bowman's capsule: that is filtration."},{"q":"Why are the PCT, loop of Henle and DCT surrounded by so many capillaries? (2 points)","right":"Reabsorption into the blood, and secretion of wastes into tubules","wrong":["Reabsorption into the blood, and making ADH for the tubules","Pushing proteins into tubules, and secretion of wastes into them","Carrying urine to the bladder, and making urea from amino acids"],"why":"Two points: reabsorption (water, glucose and salts go back into the blood) and secretion (extra wastes pass from the blood into the tubules)."},{"q":"How does more ADH change urine volume?","right":"Lowers it: more water is reabsorbed","wrong":["Raises it: less water is reabsorbed","Lowers it: less water is filtered","Raises it: more salt is excreted"],"why":"ADH makes the collecting duct more permeable, so more water returns to the blood and a small volume of urine forms."},{"q":"Urine leaves C and travels to the bladder through which tube?","right":"Ureter","wrong":["Urethra","Collecting duct","Renal vein"],"why":"The ureter carries urine from the kidney's pelvis to the bladder; the urethra takes it from the bladder out."}],"2023-Q6d":[{"q":"Why is urine volume low if you don't drink much?","right":"Collecting ducts reabsorb more water","wrong":["Collecting ducts reabsorb less water","The pituitary releases less ADH","Bowman's capsule filters more water"],"why":"Low water in the blood triggers more ADH, so the collecting ducts reabsorb more water and less urine is made."}],"2023-Q17c":[{"q":"Which vein carries blood with the least metabolic waste?","right":"Renal vein","wrong":["Hepatic vein","Hepatic portal vein","Inferior vena cava"],"why":"Blood leaving the kidneys has just had urea and other wastes filtered out, so the renal vein has the least."}],"2024-Q17c":[{"q":"What is the name for keeping a constant internal environment?","right":"Homeostasis","wrong":["Excretion","Osmoregulation","Metabolism"],"why":"Homeostasis is the general term; osmoregulation is just the water and salt part of it."},{"q":"On a nephron diagram, which structure cups the glomerulus?","right":"Bowman's capsule","wrong":["Loop of Henle","Collecting duct","Distal tubule"],"why":"Label it with the others: Bowman's capsule (the cup round the glomerulus), glomerulus, PCT, loop of Henle, DCT, collecting duct."},{"q":"Which is a way blood differs from glomerular filtrate?","right":"Blood has cells; filtrate has none","wrong":["Filtrate has cells; blood has none","Blood has glucose; filtrate has none","Blood has urea; filtrate has none"],"why":"Blood cells (and plasma proteins) are too big to be filtered; glucose and urea pass into the filtrate."},{"q":"Which part of the nephron reabsorbs water?","right":"Loop of Henle","wrong":["Glomerulus","Bowman's capsule","Renal pelvis"],"why":"Water is reabsorbed along the tubules (PCT, loop, DCT, collecting duct); the glomerulus is where filtering happens."}],"2025-Q17c":[{"q":"What is homeostasis?","right":"Maintaining a constant internal environment","wrong":["Maintaining a constant rate of growth","Removing metabolic waste from the body","Maintaining a constant external environment"],"why":"Homeostasis keeps internal conditions like temperature, pH and water constant; removing waste is excretion."},{"q":"Which endocrine gland secretes ADH?","right":"Pituitary gland","wrong":["Adrenal gland","Thyroid gland","Pineal gland"],"why":"ADH is released from the pituitary at the base of the brain; the adrenal glands make adrenaline."},{"q":"Which part of the nephron does ADH affect?","right":"Distal convoluted tubule","wrong":["Proximal convoluted tubule","Bowman's capsule","Glomerulus"],"why":"ADH acts on the distal tubule and collecting duct, making their walls let more water back into the blood."},{"q":"What does more ADH do to the volume of urine?","right":"Less urine is produced","wrong":["More urine is produced","The volume stays the same","More dilute urine is produced"],"why":"More ADH means more water is reabsorbed, so a smaller volume of more concentrated urine is made."},{"q":"From the graphs, how do blood volume and ADH change during exercise?","right":"Blood volume falls as ADH rises","wrong":["Blood volume rises as ADH rises","Blood volume falls as ADH falls","Blood volume falls, ADH stays level"],"why":"Sweating cuts blood volume, so more ADH is released to save water: volume goes down while ADH goes up."},{"q":"How can the skin help the athlete's homeostasis during exercise? (2 points)","right":"Sweating, and widening of skin blood vessels (vasodilation)","wrong":["Shivering, and absorbing water back in through the skin","Releasing ADH, and making extra urea in the skin cells","Releasing insulin, and storing extra glucose as glycogen"],"why":"Two points: sweating (evaporation carries heat away) and vasodilation (wider skin vessels lose more heat). Shivering is done by muscles."}],"2026-Q17b":[{"q":"What is the ability to keep a constant internal environment called?","right":"Homeostasis","wrong":["Excretion","Thermoregulation","Metabolism"],"why":"Homeostasis covers all internal conditions; thermoregulation only covers temperature."},{"q":"Why must organisms keep a constant internal environment?","right":"So enzymes can work efficiently","wrong":["So body temperature can match the air","So no waste is ever produced","So cells can take in water freely"],"why":"Enzymes need the right temperature and pH; keeping these constant lets metabolism run efficiently."}],"2026-Q17c":[{"q":"What does excretion mean?","right":"Removing the waste products of metabolism","wrong":["Removing fibre and undigested food","Releasing enzymes from a gland","Taking useful substances back into blood"],"why":"Excretion removes waste made by the body's chemical reactions, like urea; removing fibre is egestion."},{"q":"Which way should the urine arrow on the ureter point?","right":"From kidney to bladder","wrong":["From bladder to kidney","From bladder to urethra","From kidney to urethra"],"why":"Urine is made in the kidney and flows down the ureter into the bladder; the urethra then carries it out."},{"q":"In part B (loop of Henle), what happens?","right":"Water and salts are reabsorbed","wrong":["Blood is filtered under pressure","ADH is made and sent to the blood","Glucose is moved into the urine"],"why":"The loop of Henle reabsorbs water and salts; filtering under pressure happens at A, Bowman's capsule."},{"q":"Why is there normally no protein in urine?","right":"Protein is too big to be filtered","wrong":["ADH stops protein being filtered","Protein is turned into urea first","Protein is used up by kidney cells"],"why":"Protein molecules are too big to pass through the glomerulus wall into Bowman's capsule, so they stay in the blood."}],"S1-Q1e":[{"q":"On the diagram, which part is A?","right":"Proximal convoluted tubule","wrong":["Distal convoluted tubule","Collecting duct","Bowman's capsule"],"why":"A comes straight after Bowman's capsule, before the loop of Henle, so it is the proximal (first) tubule."},{"q":"Where on the diagram should you write F for ultrafiltration?","right":"On the glomerulus","wrong":["On the loop of Henle","On the collecting duct","On the distal tubule"],"why":"Ultrafiltration happens where blood under pressure in the glomerulus is squeezed into Bowman's capsule."},{"q":"Which part of the blood is not normally found in urine?","right":"Plasma proteins","wrong":["Urea","Water","Mineral salts"],"why":"Plasma proteins are too big to be filtered; urea, water and salts all end up in urine."},{"q":"How does a longer loop of Henle help a desert animal?","right":"More water is reabsorbed into the blood","wrong":["More water is lost in dilute urine","Blood is filtered at a higher pressure","More glucose is reabsorbed into the blood"],"why":"A longer loop lets more water be reabsorbed, so the animal makes concentrated urine and saves water."}]};
